  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Wed May 27 2026\n\nShortwave ridging is expected to result in quiet weather for \nThursday night through most of Friday. By Friday night, a \ndeepening upper-level trough over California will move into the \nFour Corners region. A weak wave ahead of this feature will move \ninto west Texas and place the region under southwest flow aloft \nthrough Saturday. A warm and moist low level airmass should also \npersist with dewpoints in the 60s. This will lead to low to medium\nchances for thunderstorms starting over the Permian Basin Friday \nevening which could push into our western counties. The region \nlooks to remain in an active, southwest flow pattern through the \nweekend. This should lead to additional rounds of thunderstorms on\nSaturday night and Sunday night with otherwise near-normal \ntemperatures. The main upper-level trough over the western US \nshould lift to the northern Plains by early next week. The pattern\nwill become a bit murky for the first half of next week. While \nsome ridging looks to build in across west Texas, it will be \ntempered a bit by the development of a shortwave trough over \ncentral Texas. Models show a \"back-door\" frontal boundary also \nmoving through central Texas Monday through Wednesday. A warm and \nmoist pattern should also continue which would lead to more \nchances for showers and thunderstorms during this time. However, \ngiven the uncertainty of timing and location of these key \nfeatures, overall rain chances were kept low as storms should be \nscattered in nature.",
